# Variants

A book written in English reaches the readers who read English. An Estonian reader of the same book wants its sections in Estonian, under Estonian titles, beside the same code.

A variant is a section written for readers in one context, such as a locale, that gives another section's content to those readers. A locale is what a reader reads in: Estonian, say, or Portuguese as Brazil writes it. The section a variant varies is the canonical section. Every reader sees the canonical section's text unless a variant fits them better.

## A section for other readers

A variant is a section. Its heading carries the variant's own title, and its specifier names the section it varies and the context it is for:

```
## Sleeping monsters

The monsters sleep until the player enters the room.

## Magavad koletised {varies="Sleeping monsters" locale=et}

Koletised magavad, kuni mängija tuppa astub.
```

The second section is the first one's text for Estonian readers. Its specifier follows the grammar that the chapter "Parameters in braces" gives every specifier. It names neither a kind nor a language, because a variant is the same kind of section as the section it varies.

The `varies` parameter is a reference, as an anchor is. It resolves by name, nearest first, and renaming the canonical section rewrites it. A name that resolves to nothing is a fault. So is a name that resolves to another variant, since a variant always varies a canonical section.

The `locale` parameter names the locale a variant is written for, with a tag such as `et` for Estonian or `pt-BR` for Brazilian Portuguese. The tags follow BCP 47, the standard that HTML's `lang` attribute and JavaScript's `Intl.Locale` both read.

Loom keeps the word language for the language that code is written in. A section's specifier names it in the `lang` parameter, as `{Bash}` does in its short form, and the frontmatter's `Language` field names it for a whole chapter. What a reader reads is a locale. The two never share a word, so a variant's specifier never seems to name the language of its code.

Two variants of one section for one context are a fault, because no reader could be shown both.

## A variant holds no code

A variant holds prose, and boxes of its own, such as a diagram drawn with Estonian labels. It holds no code, and code in a variant is a fault.

A variant of code would turn one program into another, which lacks what the first one has. Code that changes with its context belongs to the program itself, as a section of its own that a flag or a test's layer chooses. A mock is such a section: a test's Effect layer puts the mock where the real service would stand. Build systems choose code the same way, from inside the program. Rust's `cfg` attribute, Cargo's features, and Bazel's `select()` function each pick code by a setting, and none of them gives a reader a different text.

So a reader in a variant's context reads the variant's prose beside the canonical section's code. The program stays the same for every reader, and only the words around it change.

## A variant beside its section

A variant usually stands directly after the section it varies, so a translator reads the two together. A variant opens no branch of the book's tree. The sections after it nest as if it were not there. A variant takes no number, and the table of contents shows its title only to readers in its context.

A variant covers its section's own text, and none of the sections nested under it. Each nested section has variants of its own. An author who wants a translation to follow the text closely therefore writes short sections.

A variant's title is a name too, and it resolves to the canonical section. Estonian prose that links `::[Magavad koletised]` reaches the same section as English prose that links `::[Sleeping monsters]`. An anchor in code that names a variant is a fault, since a variant holds no code to compose.

## The weave chooses for the reader

The weave writes the book for one context at a time. For each section, it takes the variant that fits the reader's context, and the canonical section where no variant fits. A reader of the Estonian book reads Estonian wherever a variant exists, and English wherever none exists yet.

In the editor, a badge beside a section's heading names each locale the section has a variant for. The chapter "Decorators" describes how the editor draws it.

## Knowing when a variant falls behind

A canonical section keeps changing after its variants are written, and a variant made from an older text says what the section no longer says. A variant therefore records the text it was made from. Its `from` parameter holds a short fingerprint of the canonical section's prose and boxes:

```
## Magavad koletised {varies="Sleeping monsters" locale=et from=3f9a2c}
```

The editor writes the fingerprint when the author marks the variant up to date. When the canonical section's prose or boxes change, the fingerprint stops matching, and the editor marks the variant as behind until someone brings it up to date. A change to the canonical section's code alone leaves its variants current, since no variant holds that code.

The fingerprint lives in the text, so it works the same on a disk and in remote storage. Neither of them needs to keep a history for it.

## What to work out

- **Contexts beyond locale.** A variant for beginners, or for a reader on a small screen, needs a parameter beside `locale`. Which contexts Loom knows, and how a reader's context chooses among several variants, are open.
- **A loom per locale.** A translator may prefer one loom per locale to variants spread through every chapter. The `varies` parameter resolves like an anchor, so it can name a section of another chapter by a path. Where such a loom sits in the book, and what its frontmatter says, is open.
- **Prose among the code.** A section that alternates prose and code has several runs of prose, and its variant has one. How the woven page sets the variant's prose among the canonical section's code blocks is open.
- **The fingerprint.** How many characters the `from` fingerprint keeps, and which hash makes it, is open.

## What this chapter grows into

1. Variant sections: the `varies` and `locale` parameters, the reference that `varies` makes, and the faults for a missing section, a variant of a variant, two variants for one context, and code in a variant.
2. A variant's title as a name for its canonical section, and the fault for an anchor in code that names a variant.
3. The weave for one context, which picks the variant that fits each section.
4. The `from` fingerprint, which the editor writes and checks against the canonical section's prose and boxes.
5. The badges that name a section's variants, and the mark on a variant that has fallen behind.

When variants parse, resolve, and weave for a reader's locale, this chapter joins Part II, The shape of a loom, and the chapters that weave the book.
